# DESCRIPTION

k1-pinctrl drives the SpacemiT K1's pin controller, at `0xd401e000`, which sets each of its 128 pins' function and its pad: its pull up or down, drive strength, Schmitt trigger and slew rate.
devmgr(8) starts it after the APBC's k1-syscon(3), whose clocks it turns on, and before the devices that link to it, by its record:

```text
driver k1-pinctrl
class pinctrl
match dt spacemit,k1-pinctrl
```

Its states are the nodes under its own, read from its properties at `/dev/dt/node`: `gmac1-cfg`, each with groups whose `pinmux` lists `K1_PADCONF(pin, function)` and whose `bias-disable`, `bias-pull-up`, `bias-pull-down`, `drive-strength`, `input-schmitt`, `power-source` and `slew-rate` set its pins' pads, as Linux reads them.
A device's `pinctrl-<n>` names a state, and devmgr gives its driver this tree at `/dev/provider/<service>`; a driver sets its `default` state before it starts its device, as k1-emac(3) does.
Setting a state writes each of its pins' registers whole: the function and the group's settings, nothing else kept.
`bias-pull-up` with no value is a strong pull, as Linux takes it.
A drive strength in mA becomes the nearest code at or above it for the pin's voltage: 1.8 V for most pins, and the group's `power-source`, 1.8 or 3.3 V, for those powered from outside (pins 47 to 52, 75 to 80 and 98 to 109, the SD card's and QSPI's).
The SD card's pads (pins 104 to 109) are also in an I/O power domain, 1.8 or 3.3 V: a state whose group of those pins has a `power-source` sets the domain to it before its pins, as Linux does when an SD driver switches a card to 1.8 V (sdhci(3) would choose `mmc1-uhs-cfg` for UHS, and `mmc1-cfg` back).
The domain's register is protected: each access follows the APBC's keys, written through k1-syscon(3)'s `unlock`, the APBC the controller's node names in `spacemit,apbc`.
The write is its 1.8 V bit (bit 2) alone, the other bits being reserved, as Linux writes it: 3.3 V is 0; on the Orange Pi RV2 it reads `0x89` from reset.
Each write is read back, unlocked again, and a bit that didn't take fails the state as the keys do; the log's first line gives the register as it was, `SD pads' power domain 0x<n>`.
Where the keys can't be written, a 1.8 V state fails, and a 3.3 V one, the pads' state from reset, is set without its domain and the failure noted.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/k1-pinctrl-dt-pinctrl@d401e000`, and at `/dev/provider/<service>` in the drivers of the devices that link to it.

: `ctl`
  Takes a command a line, `state <name>`: each of the state's pins set.
  A line it can't parse, a state it doesn't have, a drive strength on outside-powered pins whose group gives no `power-source`, or a 1.8 V state whose power domain the APBC doesn't unlock fails the write as invalid, and the driver logs which.
: `status`
  Read only.
  `pins 128, states <n>`; then a line for each state, `state gmac1-cfg 15 pins set`, or `not set` where any of its pins' registers reads otherwise now; then `sd pads 1.8 V` or `3.3 V`, as last set, once a state has set them; then `commands <n>, failed <n>`, and `last failure: state <name>: <why>`.

